  "answer_text": "This is a famous quote by the 14th-century North African polymath, sociologist, and historian **Ibn Khaldun**, written in his monumental 1377 work, ***The Muqaddimah*** *(An Introduction to History)*. \n\nThe complete sentence is:\n\n> **\"Businesses owned by responsible and organized merchants will eventually surpass those owned by wealthy rulers.\"**\n\n### Context and Meaning\nIn *The Muqaddimah*, Ibn Khaldun laid down the foundations for modern economics, sociology, and historiography centuries before European thinkers like Adam Smith or Karl Marx. \n\nThis specific quote touches on the **danger of state-run commerce and monopolies**. Ibn Khaldun argued that when a ruler or government enters the marketplace to engage in trade and business, it disrupts the natural economy. His reasoning was twofold:\n\n1. **Unfair Competition:** Rulers hold coercive power. They can avoid taxes, force lower prices from producers, and dictate terms to buyers. This pushes private, independent merchants out of the market.\n2. **Inefficiency and Neglect:** Wealthy rulers do not run businesses based on efficiency, customer satisfaction, or careful financial management; they rely on their vast state coffers and political power. Private, \"responsible and organized\" merchants, on the other hand, must remain sharp, calculated, and customer-focused to survive. \n\nEventually, the private sector's adaptability, organization, and adherence to market forces allow it to build more sustainable, resilient, and prosperous enterprises than those propped up by the sheer, unearned wealth of state rulers. \n\n### Modern Relevance\nIbn Khaldun's observation is widely seen as an early endorsement of free-market economics, property rights, and the separation of government and private commerce. It warns against state capitalism and central planning, emphasizing that the most enduring economic strength comes from a thriving, organized, and independent private sector.",
